      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{3}{2}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{3}{2}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](generate/scratch/17592186096649-draft/plots/d2db8597f80794959956f4e9e458a0f81beb80c4b8f909bcfdcf967d30e0898b.png)"
