      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that both $2a$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ must be parallel to $a$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $2a$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\nNext, for the vector $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$.\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](generate/scratch/17592186096649-draft/plots/61cd16cacebaf0072590d125e1db70e91d66f91015305796e9f3771a136048fb.png)"
